title touchstone — the loop that has to get better

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  READ THIS FIRST — three conventions, and the measurements that set them.
//
//  1 · WHERE A NODE CARRIES A PHASE COLOUR, THAT COLOUR IS THE SCHEDULE.
//      A node coloured for a phase it is not in is the quiet lie the key exists
//      to stop. The schedule is most of the risk, so that is the colour that has
//      to be right.
//
//      ⚠ THIS RULE READ "COLOUR IS PHASE, AND NOTHING ELSE — ONE exception:
//        section 11" UNTIL 2026-08-16, AND THAT WAS MEASURABLY FALSE. Counted
//        off the DSL rather than remembered:
//          · red    12 nodes, NOT ONE of them a phase — and it has had its own
//                   legend row the whole time ("an invariant, or a never"). So
//                   the biggest exception was in the key, and the header still
//                   said there was one exception and named a different one.
//          · grey    7 nodes, and only Life and V5 are "deferred, or cut".
//                   Boundary, Voided, Incomplete, Admit and C5 are none of those.
//          · none   39 nodes carry no colour at all, so the phase is read off
//                   the Pn.n in the label — which is the real mechanism, and
//                   convention 3 is where it is actually written down.
//        ⚠ RE-COUNTED 2026-08-16 after section 14 landed: 86 declarations, and
//          the three figures above moved by +2 / +1 / +5 in one pass. They were
//          10 / 6 / 34 an hour earlier. ⛔ A census written into prose goes stale
//          on the NEXT edit — re-derive it from the DSL before quoting it, never
//          step it by hand. The command is in diagrams/README.md.
//        ⛔ The rule was not wrong when written; it was written about the
//           schedule colours and then read as a claim about every colour. A
//           convention stated more strongly than it is true gets enforced
//           somewhere it does not apply, which is how ApiPy ended up with a
//           comment describing a colour it did not have (DEF-013).
//
//      ✅ GREEN WAS THE ONE WRONG COLOUR AND IT IS SETTLED — D-053, 2026-08-18.
//        It carried the key "phase 0 — shipped" while sitting on `Scored` and
//        `Promoted`, the two GOOD OUTCOMES, neither of them phase 0. `DoctorPy`
//        then made it sharper rather than better: one true P0 node and two
//        outcomes wearing one colour.
//        ⛔ THE KEY GAVE WAY, NOT THE NODES. It now reads "phase 0 — shipped, or
//        a good outcome", which is a documented second meaning instead of an
//        undocumented one. Stripping green off the outcomes was the tidier fix
//        and was rejected: the good path reading at a glance is worth more here
//        than the one-colour-one-meaning rule, and the rule was never absolute —
//        red and purple already have their own rows, as four lines below say.
//        ⚠ WHAT THIS COSTS, SAID PLAINLY: green can no longer be read as a
//        schedule claim on its own, so a green node needs its label checked. That
//        is a real loss and it is the price of the choice, not an argument that
//        the choice was free. ⛔ It applies to GREEN ALONE. Any other colour
//        acquiring a second meaning is DEF-013 recurring, and the giveaway is
//        that the key will not mention it.
//
//      TWO documented non-phase uses, both stated where they are used:
//        · section 11, where colour is case STATUS. It says so there.
//        · red and purple, which have their own legend rows.
//
//  2 · THE BOXES CARRY THE NAME AND THE RULE. THESE COMMENTS CARRY THE ARGUMENT.
//      ⛔ ONE line a node. TWO at the outside. If it needs a paragraph it is an
//         argument, and arguments live down here, where they cost no pixels.
//      The reason is READABILITY: one line is what a reader takes in from a box
//      at a glance, and a box holding a paragraph is a box nobody reads. That
//      alone carries the rule — which matters, because the OTHER reason once
//      given for it turned out to be measuring something else entirely.
//
//      ⚠ EVERY BEFORE/AFTER PIXEL FIGURE THAT ONCE SAT HERE IS WITHDRAWN — NOT
//        CORRECTED, WITHDRAWN — AND SO IS THE MECHANISM THEY ARGUED FOR. The
//        claim was that fat labels act as walls the auto-layout routes around,
//        so prose in one box inflates the whole canvas. Every number supporting
//        it was taken one-export-after-one-push on a diagram that had been
//        updated many times. ⛔ MEASURED 2026-08-15, that is a confound, not a
//        result: ONE DSL, rendered TWICE, came out 6388 x 12786 (81.7 MP) in a
//        newly-created diagram and 9338 x 16401 (153.2 MP) in one that had been
//        updated ~8 times. Same bytes. 88% more area. The variable was the
//        NUMBER OF EDITS, and it was the variable nobody was controlling.
//        ⚠ That pair is of itself. It was taken just before the specialist
//          labels gained their tool line, so it does NOT compare to the settled
//          figure in diagrams/README.md — a pair is only a pair against the DSL
//          it was measured on, which is what the withdrawn figures got wrong.
//
//  ⛔ THE RULE THAT REPLACES THEM — RE-CREATE BEFORE YOU EXPORT.
//     A hosted Eraser diagram keeps its element positions across updates and
//     only lays out fully when it is CREATED. So an old diagram's canvas is a
//     record of its edit history, and no amount of editing walks it back.
//     Therefore: `manually_create_diagram` from THIS FILE and export from the
//     new one. ⛔ NEVER `manually_update_diagram`, and never export from a
//     diagram you have been editing.
//
//     ⛔ AND THE HOSTED DIAGRAM IS REPLACED, NOT LEFT BEHIND. Delete the old
//        one, create the new one, KEEP it. The rule here used to end "delete
//        it", which optimised for the export and quietly let the workspace go
//        stale: on 2026-08-16 the hosted copy sat seven fixes behind this file,
//        and it is the copy that LOOKS canonical when someone opens the
//        workspace. A renderer nobody has marked stale is worse than no
//        renderer. Delete-and-recreate is the same call sequence as before with
//        the delete moved to the front, so it costs nothing and keeps both
//        copies honest.
//     ⚠ THE TRIGGER IS A CHANGE TO RENDERED CONTENT, NOT A CHANGE TO THIS FILE.
//        Comments do not render. Strip `//` lines from both versions and compare;
//        if they match, the PNG and the hosted copy are both still correct and
//        re-uploading buys nothing. That check is what proved this very edit
//        needed no re-export.
//     📐 The file ID is stable across a replace and only the diagram ID changes,
//        so a workspace bookmark keeps working.
//     Cheap, because this file is the artifact and the hosted copy is a
//     renderer — see docs/07 §5 rule 1.
//     ⛔ AND WHEN MEASURING ANYTHING: export TWICE, compare the returned URLs.
//        They are content-addressed (elements:<sha256>.png), so an identical
//        hash means the render settled; a first export can return the PREVIOUS
//        one. Two exports of one unchanged diagram came back 82.6 MP and
//        156.2 MP before this was understood.
//     🎯 THE POINT IS NOT THE PIXELS. A number was quoted for three drafts, in
//        a file whose whole subject is refusing unearned claims, and it was
//        measuring the measurement apparatus. That is this project's own thesis
//        landing on its own documents — which is why the wrong version is
//        recorded here rather than quietly replaced.
//
//  ⛔ AND NO ⛔ OR 🔴 INSIDE A LABEL — MEASURED 2026-08-18, DEF-027. Eraser's render
//     font carries no colour emoji, so both of those fall back to ONE identical
//     featureless bar: `🔴 THE WEAKEST GATE` came out `▮ THE WEAKEST GATE` on P2Gate and
//     `⛔ CUT by D-052` came out `▮ CUT by D-052` on ApiPy. Two marks meaning different
//     things — a never, and a warning about this project — collapse into one mark
//     meaning nothing, and the reader cannot even tell which was intended.
//     ⚠ IN THIS FILE IT IS NOT THE SILENT-DROP FAMILY, AND THAT IS WHY IT SURVIVED. A swallowed
//       message leaves a hole a grep can find (milestone 8). This leaves the sentence
//       intact and mangles one character inside it, so the DSL round-trips, the hash
//       settles, the extent check reads 98.4% — every instrument passes a picture with
//       nine bars in it. It was caught by LOOKING, same as DEF-020 and DEF-026.
//     ⛔ THE BLOCKLIST IS TWO CHARACTERS AND MUST STAY TWO. Measured on the same render:
//       ⚠ renders as a real warning triangle, ✅ as a check, and → ≥ · — – all render
//       correctly. Widening this to "no emoji in labels" would strip glyphs that WORK,
//       on the theory that they are the same kind of thing. They are not — the
//       measurement is the boundary, not the intuition.
//     ⛔ AND THE BAR IS A FLOWCHART BEHAVIOUR ONLY. The same two characters in
//       diagrams/sequence.eraser render as ZERO pixels — a silent drop, the `note
//       over` family. One defect, two failure modes; a glyph checked in one file
//       is unchecked in the other, which is why milestone 9 reads BOTH files.
//     THESE COMMENTS KEEP THEIR GLYPHS. They render no pixels, so nothing here is at
//     risk; the rule binds the ~102 rendered labels only, and milestone 9 enforces it.
//
//  3 · EVERY NODE NAMES SOMETHING REAL — a path in docs/09 §9's file map, a key
//      in AgentState, or a status in docs/09 §6. If a name here cannot be
//      pointed at in this repo, it does not belong in this diagram.
//
//  ⛔ THE LOAD-BEARING ABSENCES. Four things are deliberately NOT drawn. Each is
//     a design decision, not an omission, and each would be invisible as a
//     missing line — so they are listed rather than left to be noticed:
//
//       · NO arrow from Agent to ScorePy. The scorer reads spans, never a return
//         value (D-007). Draw that arrow and CI can no longer score a committed
//         trace with no credential — and nothing would fail visibly.
//       · NO arrow out of SLocked back to SOpen. locked is one-way. That absence
//         IS the ratchet; every other property here follows from it.
//       · NO arrow from Truth to anything but ScorePy. Invariant 1.
//       · NO arrow from Bench to the gate. The gate compares hashes carried in
//         manifest.json (condition 1) — it never re-reads the cases. An arrow
//         there would imply the gate can see the corpus at compare time.
//
//  📐 WHY AgentState IS A SIBLING GROUP AND NOT NESTED INSIDE Agent.
//     agent/state.py IS inside agent/, so nesting it was tried — on the argument
//     that the drawing should follow the file tree. It was reverted for a reason
//     that has nothing to do with size: nested, the layout put the three
//     specialists ABOVE the supervisor that routes to them. A container that
//     inverts the reading order costs more than the containment buys, and that
//     is a defect you can SEE in the render rather than a number off an export.
//     Sibling it is, and the edges into it carry the relationship instead.
//     ⚠ The canvas figures once quoted here were withdrawn with all the others
//       (convention 2). Nothing in this argument depended on them, which is why
//       the conclusion did not move.
//     ⚠ Recorded because the argument for nesting is a good one and will be made
//       again — by someone reading docs/09 §9, reasonably. This is the answer.
//
//  📐 `direction right` WAS TRIED AND DOES NOTHING HERE. The reasoning was good
//     and the measurement killed it. The argument: a node box is ~3x wider than
//     it is tall, so laying out DOWN makes each rank's width a function of LABEL
//     WIDTH, while RIGHT would make it a function of NODE COUNT — and the deep
//     chains (C1..C5, V1..V5, the corpus) should then stop stretching the page.
//     MEASURED under the two-export settle rule: the two renders have a
//     PIXEL-IDENTICAL content bounding box, 9338 x 16401, and differ in 0.237%
//     of pixels — 0.031% by more than a hair. Whatever sets this drawing's
//     extent, it is not the flow direction; grouping and edge routing dominate
//     and `direction` only nudges a few connectors. Left at `down`, which is
//     the order the sections are numbered in.
//     ⚠ That rationale was WRITTEN BEFORE THE MEASUREMENT and is kept only as
//       the record of a disproof. Do not re-derive it as a reason to switch.
//
//  📐 WHAT THE RETIRED HTML POSTER CARRIED, AND WHERE EACH PART WENT (D-036).
//     diagrams/touchstone.html drew the same system in ten sections and six
//     encodings. Deleting it is only honest if every section has somewhere to
//     be, so this is that list — written BEFORE the delete, not after.
//       1 the spine              -> sections 1-9 here, as nodes and edges
//       2 the agent graph        -> section 2
//       3 who may call what      -> the specialist labels in section 2 now name
//                                   their OWN two tools. See below.
//       4 the state              -> section 3
//       5 how an attempt ends    -> section 6
//       6 the trace              -> sections 7-8. NOT as a flame graph.
//       7 the gate, all five     -> section 10, C1..C5 as a chain
//         open -> locked         -> section 11, as the state machine it is
//       8 the version ladder     -> section 12
//       9 layout and boundaries  -> section 5, and docs/09 §9 owns the tree
//      10 what exists when       -> the phase colours in the legend, plus a
//                                   Pn.n on every label. ROADMAP.md is the
//                                   authority; a second timeline would drift.
//     TWO things are NOT carried, and both already had an owner:
//       · the SHAPE of a trace — docs/04 defines the span vocabulary, and a
//         drawing of nesting cannot be checked against anything.
//       · the seven invariants strip — ⛔ docs/01 §6 has FOURTEEN, and the
//         poster said so on its own face: "that list has fourteen and this is
//         a selection." A partial second copy of a checkable list is the drift
//         D-036 exists to stop. The ones that are STRUCTURAL are drawn where
//         they bite: truth.json red and outside the agent, findings as the one
//         reduced key, HEADERS ONLY on the supervisor edge, locked one-way.
//
//  📐 SECTION 3 OF THE POSTER WAS A MATRIX, AND A MATRIX SAYS SOMETHING A
//     GRAPH CANNOT: an EMPTY CELL is a claim — this specialist may NOT reach
//     that tool — whereas a missing arrow is indistinguishable from an arrow
//     nobody drew. Six edges from three specialists to four tools also cross,
//     which is what made it a matrix in the first place.
//     ⛔ SO IT IS FLATTENED INTO THE LABELS, NOT DROPPED: each specialist
//        names its own two (docs/03 §135-137), read.py says there are four,
//        and three names of two out of four IS the matrix — a reader can
//        reconstruct every cell, including the empty ones. The single edge
//        `Specialists > ReadPy: two each` carries the count so the pairing
//        cannot silently grow.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

direction down
colorMode pastel
styleMode shadow
typeface clean

legend [position: top-left] {
  // ⚠ TWO MEANINGS, ON PURPOSE, AND THAT IS D-053 RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT. Convention 1
  //    says a colour is the schedule; this row breaks it deliberately, which is why the key
  //    now says so out loud instead of leaving a reader to find `Scored` and wonder. The
  //    alternative — strip green off the two outcomes — was rejected because the good path
  //    reading at a glance is worth more here than one colour meaning one thing.
  //    ⛔ IT IS NOT A LICENCE. Green is the ONLY colour with two meanings, both named in
  //    the key. A second colour doing this quietly is the DEF-013 failure again.
  [color: green, label: "phase 0 — shipped, or a good outcome"]
  [color: blue, label: "phase 1 — next"]
  [color: orange, label: "phase 2 — v0.1.0"]
  [color: pink, label: "phase 3 — v0.2.0"]
  [color: grey, label: "deferred, or cut"]
  [color: red, label: "an invariant, or a never"]
  // D-047, and the citation is in this comment rather than in the label on purpose: the
  // label is rendered text, and adding to it would invalidate the PNG beside this file.
  // ⛔ This row is not decoration — it closes a gap this file had ALREADY NAMED and then
  //    worked around. The comment above `Bound`, at the foot of this file, says: "the old
  //    node was grey while it was an open question, WHICH THE KEY NEVER COVERED". It solved
  //    that one node by picking red and explaining why, and left the hole open. api.py is
  //    the next one, and it was drawn in the default palette while its comment claimed it
  //    was "grey-question form" — the prose and the render disagreeing, DEF-011's shape.
  //    ⚠ "Deferred" and "undecided" are different states and must not share a colour:
  //    deferred means the answer is yes and the date is later; this means nobody has said
  //    yes. Collapsing them is how a question quietly becomes a queued task.
  //    ⚠ AS OF 2026-08-18 THIS ROW HAS NO INSTANCE — `api.py` was the only purple node
  //    and D-053 cut it. The row STAYS: a legend defines the notation, it is not a
  //    census of what is used today, and the next open question should not have to
  //    re-argue D-047 to get a colour. ⛔ It is not a spare colour to reassign.
  [color: purple, label: "an OPEN QUESTION — nobody has decided yet"]
  [connection: -->, label: "reads it — does not trigger it"]
}

// D-044 belongs on this node and nowhere else. The developer is the ONLY writer of a
// candidate — there is no auto-tuner, and the reason is attribution (D-013), not principle.
// Saying it here rather than in a floating note keeps it attached to the thing it constrains.
Dev [shape: oval, color: blue, icon: user, label: "the developer changes exactly ONE thing\nNO auto-tuner — a human writes every candidate (D-044)"]

// ── 1 · THE CORPUS — built BEFORE the agent exists ────────────────────────────
// That ordering is the falsifiability claim. The answer key is planted before
// there is anything to grade, so "correct" is a lookup and never a judgement.

Corpus [color: blue, icon: package, label: "1 · the frozen corpus — P1.1–P1.3"] {
  DomainPy [icon: file-text, label: "domain.py — P1.1\npydantic models, docs/09 §1–4"]
  GenPy [icon: settings, label: "incidents/generate.py — P1.2\ntruth FIRST, then render"]
  RendPy [icon: layers, label: "incidents/renderers.py — P1.2\nten cause renderers + the deletion path"]
  Bench [shape: document, icon: box, label: "suite/benchmark/ — P1.3\nn=10, frozen · manifest.json\nNO writer after generation — the asymmetry is in section 11"]
  // D-042: required_specialist is drawn here because the field has a DEADLINE, not
  // because it is interesting. truth.json is hashed byte for byte, so the key is free
  // at P1.2 and a benchmark version bump at any later moment. An implementer working
  // from this picture has to see it before P1.3 freezes the tier.
  Truth [shape: document, color: red, icon: lock, label: "truth.json — INSIDE the hash · one PER TIER\nNEVER seen by the agent\n+ required_specialist per case — BEFORE P1.3 (D-042)"]
  // ⛔ THIS IS THE HALLUCINATION CHECK, AND IT IS A CLASS RATHER THAN A CHECKER — which is
  //    why a search for one finds nothing. `insufficient_evidence` appeared ZERO times in this
  //    file while docs/01 §3 calls it "the most important class", and phase 1's exit gate
  //    names case 09 specifically to force it into the suite.
  //    🎯 The design is the interesting part: there is NO hallucination detector anywhere in
  //    this system, on purpose. A detector is a second model with a second failure mode and
  //    nothing to check IT against. Instead the honest "I do not know" is planted in the
  //    answer key like any other cause, so refusing to guess is a CORRECT answer that a case
  //    can require — scored by exact match, no judge, same as everything else.
  //    ⚠ It follows that an agent which never says it cannot tell scores WRONG on case 09.
  //    That is the whole mechanism, and it was invisible on the canvas.
  Insuf [shape: document, color: red, icon: circle, label: "insufficient_evidence — docs/01 §3, the most important class\nthe honest 'I do not know', and it is a CORRECT answer\nNO hallucination detector anywhere — the CLASS is the check\ncase 09 requires it — named in phase 1's exit gate"]
}

DomainPy > GenPy > RendPy > Bench
GenPy > Truth
GenPy > Insuf: planted like any other cause — one of the ten (docs/01 §3)

// ── 2 · THE AGENT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v1 is Synthesizer ALONE, findings == []. Deliberately weak: a baseline that
// is already good hides every improvement that comes after it.
// ⛔ The SAME object and the SAME prompts/synthesizer.md, not a v1_oneshot.md
//    (D-038). That is what makes v2 − v1 attributable: if the decision layer is
//    byte-identical, the delta measures the investigation layer and nothing
//    else. The check runs at P1.4 — if the prompt needs an "if there are no
//    findings…" branch, these are not the same node and D-038 flips.
// ⛔ There is NO gate node here, and its absence is a decision (D-040). An
//    earlier revision drew HumanGate between Synthesizer and END, holding a
//    LangGraph interrupt() for blast_radius ≥ restart_service. It is deleted:
//    nothing in this system executes an action (docs/01 §7), and the measured
//    path auto-declined the interrupt on every suite run — so the pause only
//    ever existed on a branch no number came from. Blast radius now sets
//    verdict.escalate, which the scorer reads as an axis. A human rewrites this
//    machine; a human is never a state in it.

Agent [color: blue, icon: cpu, label: "2 · agent/ — P1.8 · graph.py · nodes.py · state.py\nStateGraph + SqliteSaver, checkpointed after EVERY node"] {
  Supervisor [shape: hexagon, icon: git-branch, label: "supervisor — routes on `next`\nONE per hop, never a fan-out (D-026)"]
  Specialists [color: blue, icon: search, label: "three specialists — each blind to the others"] {
    SpTimeline [icon: clock, label: "timeline — did something change?\nget_deploys · get_logs"]
    SpResource [icon: activity, label: "resource — what is saturated?\nget_metrics · get_logs"]
    SpDependency [icon: share-2, label: "dependency — us, or something we call?\nget_topology · get_metrics"]
  }
  Synthesizer [icon: check-square, label: "synthesizer — no tools, it DECIDES\nv1 is THIS NODE ALONE (D-038)\nsets escalate if blast_radius ≥ restart_service (D-040)"]
}

Supervisor > Specialists: one per hop
Specialists > Supervisor: hops += 1
Supervisor > Synthesizer: next == done, or hops ≥ MAX_HOPS (D-039)
// Synthesizer has no outgoing edge on purpose: it IS the terminal node (D-040).
// Where the attempt lands is section 6, reached by the group edge Agent > Ends.

// ── 3 · THE STATE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The write side is D-012: exactly one reduced key. The read side is D-025, and
// that is the one that goes wrong quietly — a key with an unintended READER
// inherits framing, and nothing in the run reports that it happened.

AgentState [color: blue, icon: box, label: "3 · agent/state.py — AgentState, docs/09 §4"] {
  KIncident [icon: file-text, label: "incident: Incident — written once"]
  KFindings [color: red, icon: plus-circle, label: "findings: Annotated[list[Finding], add]\nthe ONLY reduced key (D-012)"]
  KHops [icon: hash, label: "hops: int — supervisor only"]
  KVerdict [icon: award, label: "verdict: Verdict | None"]
}

KIncident > Supervisor: read by every node
Specialists > KFindings: append
KFindings > Supervisor: HEADERS ONLY (D-025)
KFindings > Synthesizer: the WHOLE list
Synthesizer > KVerdict
Supervisor <> KHops

// ── 4 · THE TOOLS — five, split 4 + 1 ─────────────────────────────────────────
// All five are built at P1.7. The fifth is dead weight until v3, and that is
// the entire content of v3: the delta is one tool on one node.

Tools [color: blue, icon: tool, label: "4 · tools/ — P1.7"] {
  ReadPy [icon: eye, label: "read.py — four read-only tools\nlogs · metrics · deploys · topology"]
  RunbooksPy [icon: book-open, label: "runbooks.py — search_runbooks\nBM25, 13 files: 10 real, 3 decoys"]
}

Specialists > ReadPy: two each
Supervisor > RunbooksPy: v3 onward

// ── 5 · OUT OF PROCESS — two boundaries, each a failure mode and a cost ───────

Boundary [color: grey, icon: shield, label: "5 · out of process"] {
  ModelsPy [color: blue, icon: code, label: "models.py — a FILE, ~60 lines (DEF-002)\nsetting_sources=[] · the model is PINNED (D-034)"]
  CLIProc [shape: hexagon, icon: terminal, label: "claude CLI — SUBPROCESS"]
  ApiEndpoint [shape: cylinder, icon: cloud, label: "Anthropic API — NETWORK"]
}

Agent > ModelsPy: every node call
ModelsPy > CLIProc > ApiEndpoint

// ── 6 · HOW AN ATTEMPT ENDS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The split that earns its keep is ours-versus-theirs: parse_failure is the
// agent's OWN output and scores as wrong; void is OUR infrastructure and counts
// toward nothing. Merge them and a bad week of 429s reads as a worse agent.
//
// ⛔ `budget_exceeded` LIVED IN THIS COMMENT AND NOWHERE ELSE UNTIL 2026-08-17,
//    which is the P1.4 failure recorded 120 lines below — "it lived in a comment,
//    and a comment renders no pixels" — recurring in the section that comes FIRST.
//    The line said it is orthogonal, lands beside any status, never instead of one.
//    Every word of that was true and the picture asserted none of it: a reader saw
//    four statuses and no flag. ⚠ A rule written down once does not fire on the next
//    instance by itself; the only reason this one was caught is that a reader asked
//    whether every node was present, and the answer came off the DSL rather than off
//    the prose. There is still no guard for "a concept with no node" and there cannot
//    be one, because the set of concepts is the judgement the diagram is FOR.

Ends [color: red, icon: flag, label: "6 · exactly ONE status per attempt — docs/09 §6"] {
  Scored [color: green, icon: check-circle, label: "scored — counts toward everything"]
  ParseFail [color: red, icon: x-circle, label: "parse_failure — scored WRONG\nnever retried (D-013)"]
  Voided [color: grey, icon: slash, label: "void — 429, or model changed\ncounts toward NOTHING (D-015)"]
  Incomplete [color: grey, icon: pause-circle, label: "incomplete — process died\nthe checkpoint survived"]
}

Agent > Ends: every attempt lands on exactly one

// ⛔ DRAWN OUTSIDE `Ends`, AND THE PLACEMENT IS THE WHOLE CLAIM. Inside that box it
//    would read as a fifth status, and the box is labelled "exactly ONE". It is a FLAG
//    on the attempt, not an outcome of it: docs/09 §6 carries it as a boolean beside
//    the status, docs/05 §5 counts it in the aggregate next to hops_exhausted, and
//    docs/04 §7 says `max_budget_usd` — a hard ceiling IN THE CLI, below the wrapper —
//    aborts the attempt and records it. A scored attempt can carry it; so can a void.
BudgetFlag [shape: document, color: red, icon: dollar-sign, label: "budget_exceeded — a FLAG, never a status\nlands BESIDE any of the four, NEVER instead of one\nmax_budget_usd aborts BELOW budget.py — docs/04 §7"]

