DoctorPy [color: green, icon: activity, label: "doctor.py — P0, THE FIRST FILE WRITTEN (docs/09 §9)\none of only TWO files with a fixed order — the other is domain.py\n✅ shipped: `touchstone doctor` runs today"]

RunPy [color: blue, icon: play, label: "loop/run.py — k attempts, config.K = 3 (D-030)\n--resume · attempt cache (D-015)"]
// ⚠ RESTRUCTURED by D-046. Judge used to live in section 13 with the phase-2 "surfaces"
// and Diag sat here unnumbered, so the one edge carrying the whole D-045 argument ran the
// height of the canvas and a reader had to find two nodes at opposite ends to answer "how
// is the rubric kept out of the gate?". Judge is not a surface — it is a scoring component
// that happens to be phase-2 TIMED, and this diagram groups by ROLE while COLOUR carries
// phase. So all three sit in one box and the orange node inside a blue group is the phase,
// said the way the legend already says it.
//
// 🔴 A PIXEL CLAIM SAT HERE AND IS WITHDRAWN — the regroup was written up as "3,941px of
// height gone, 27% less to scroll, which GROUP a node is in is a bigger layout input than
// how many nodes there are." Both renders came from the long-edited hosted diagram, which
// the rule ~40 lines above says is a record of edit history and not of content. The rule
// was in this file, and it was not followed. The D-046 export was ALSO CLIPPED — section
// 13 missing entirely — and two exports returned the identical hash, so the settle rule
// declared it good. Settled is not complete. diagrams/README.md findings 2a and row five.
// ⛔ The regroup stands on its reading argument alone, which is the one it was made for.
Scoring [color: blue, icon: bar-chart-2, label: "9 · scoring — what happens at score time"] {
  // Note what does NOT arrive here: the agent's return value. That absence is what lets CI
  // score a committed JSONL with no credential and no model call.
  // ⚠ Both metrics are named because the CONTRAST is the claim, and the picture carried
  // only half of it: all_k appeared four times, pass@1 not once. docs/05 §2 — "pass@1
  // describes a demo, all_k describes something you would put on call." A diagram that
  // shows only the headline number has quietly dropped the sentence that makes it mean
  // anything, and P3.1 requires both reported ESPECIALLY when they differ.
  // ⛔ AND THAT FIX WAS STILL HALF OF IT (DEF-017). docs/05 has FOUR scored axes — §1 correctness,
  //    §2 all_k, §3 escalation F1, §4 cost per correct — and `F1` appeared ZERO times in
  //    this file, comments included. Two of the four reached the canvas and one more
  //    (cost per correct) only ever appeared downstream on C4, where it reads as a budget
  //    rule rather than as something the scorer computes. Naming the SET on the node is
  //    what stops the next axis going missing: a reader can now count four and check.
  ScorePy [color: blue, icon: bar-chart-2, label: "loop/score.py — P1.6 · NO model call\nspans + truth.json → results/(version).json (D-007)\nFOUR axes, docs/05 §1–4: correctness · all_k · escalation F1 · cost per correct\nall_k is the HEADLINE · pass@1 beside it, docs/05 §2 — BOTH, especially when they differ\nTWO outputs — only ONE of them reaches the gate"]

  // The dead end IS the mechanism. The old label said "DIAGNOSTIC, never gates", which was
  // the prose promise drawn as though it were a structure. A box with no outgoing edge is
  // the only honest way to draw "nothing downstream may read this" — and it is why a rubric
  // is not a human in the loop: a human gate HALTS the machine, this cannot even be observed
  // by it. ⛔ If an edge ever leaves this node, D-045 has been broken.
  Diag [shape: document, color: red, icon: eye-off, label: "diagnostics{} — inside results/(version).json\ncompare.py NEVER reads inside it — ONE TEST proves it (D-045)\nrubric + judge_model + rubric_hash (D-041) · router P/R (D-042)\nNO OUTGOING EDGE, ON PURPOSE"]

  // Phase-2 timed, drawn here anyway: it belongs to scoring, and P2.6a says when.
